        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.722,
            0.065,
            0.834,
            0.081
          ],
          "content": "Number Play"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.157,
            0.12,
            0.834,
            0.187
          ],
          "content": "8. Recall the sequence of Powers of 2 from Chapter 1, Table 1. Why is the Collatz conjecture correct for all the starting numbers in this sequence?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.157,
            0.194,
            0.824,
            0.215
          ],
          "content": "9. Check if the Collatz Conjecture holds for the starting number 100."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.222,
            0.834,
            0.267
          ],
          "content": "10. Starting with 0, players alternate adding numbers between 1 and 3. The first person to reach 22 wins. What is the winning strategy now?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.373,
            0.315,
            0.579,
            0.341
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Summary"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.374,
            0.834,
            0.44
          ],
          "content": "Numbers can be used for many different purposes including, to convey information, make and discover patterns, estimate magnitudes, pose and solve puzzles, and play and win games."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.442,
            0.834,
            0.508
          ],
          "content": "Thinking about and formulating set procedures to use numbers for these purposes is a useful skill and capacity (called ‘computational thinking’)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.511,
            0.834,
            0.577
          ],
          "content": "Many problems about numbers can be very easy to pose, but very difficult to solve. Indeed, numerous such problems are still unsolved (for example, Collatz’s Conjecture)."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.889,
            0.942,
            0.914,
            0.959
          ],
          "content": "73"
        },
        {
